# debris_sphere.k26asm - a derelict object, not a spacecraft.
#
# This asset exists to be in the way. An environment that asks a craft
# to fly past things needs things to fly past, and this is one: a
# passive body with mass and a collider, no actuators, no docking
# interface, and no instruments. Nothing here is cited. Every number
# is chosen and every provenance line says so.
#
# WHY A SPHERE. A body that carries no attitude control holds
# whatever orientation it was given, and an environment that places
# several of these gives them all the same one, because an orientation
# is not among the state components a placement draw can reach. A box
# or a capsule would therefore lay the whole field out on one set of
# axes, and a craft could learn the grain of the field instead of
# learning to look at it. A sphere has no orientation to share, so the
# field it makes has no grain.
#
# WHAT THE NUMBERS STAND FOR. Three metres of radius and two tonnes.
# The class in mind is a spent upper stage or a comparable derelict:
# the Centaur stage is about 3.05 m across and about 2250 kg dry, so
# an object of this bulk and this mass is of that order without being
# a model of any particular one. The implied bulk density is 17.7
# kg/m^3, which is what a mostly empty structure has, and the mass is
# derived by the compiler from the collider under the assumption of a
# uniform solid, so the derived inertia is that of a uniform sphere
# and not that of a shell. It overstates nothing a task here reads.
#
# WHAT THE MASS DOES AND DOES NOT DO. It sets a gravitational
# parameter of 1.3e-7 m^3/s^2, which at a hundred metres accelerates
# another body by 1.3e-11 m/s^2 and moves nothing over any episode
# length this format is used at. It also sets what a contact does to
# the pair, and an environment that ends its episode on contact never
# advances past that moment. So the figure is honest rather than
# load bearing, and it is stated here so that an environment which
# does read it knows what it is reading.
#
# The frame declaration names the only body frame the format defines.
# Its x axis is the longitudinal axis toward a docking interface, and
# this article has neither, so the declaration fixes an axis that
# nothing here distinguishes. A sphere is invariant under every
# rotation, so no derived quantity depends on the choice.

assembly debris_sphere
    frame x_to_port

    provenance mass "two tonnes, chosen for the derelict class this stands for; the Centaur upper stage is about 2250 kg dry, which is the order this sits at and not a figure it reproduces" modelled
    provenance hull_geometry "one sphere of 3.0 m radius, chosen so that a field of these objects is a hazard at the scale of the approach it obstructs; no source publishes a shape for a generic derelict" modelled
    provenance inertia "computed from the modelled mass and sphere at uniform density" computed

    component hull
        mass 2000.0
        at 0.0 0.0 0.0
        collider sphere 0.0 0.0 0.0 3.0
    end
end
